Eosinophilic Oesophagitis
Gastrointestinal & Hepatology
Solid food dysphagia/impaction, Atopic history, Eosinophilic infiltration on biopsy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: GIH-019
Reason for Visit: Acute inability to swallow solid food, intense retrosternal chest pain, and repeated regurgitation of saliva following a meal.
Location: Private residence.
Present at Visit: Patient, partner.
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated acute solid food impaction in the oesophagus causing total inability to clear secretions (drooling/regurgitation) and severe retrosternal distress. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was strictly necessary to evaluate airway patency, attempt pharmacological relaxation of the lower oesophageal sphincter, and arrange immediate emergency endoscopic disimpaction before the onset of aspiration or mucosal necrosis.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I swallowed a piece of chicken and it stopped dead in my chest; now it hurts intensely and I have to spit my saliva into a cup."
History of Present Illness: Acute food impaction occurring 2 hours ago during dinner. Patient reports intense retrosternal chest pain and a sensation of the food bolus firmly lodged in the mid-oesophagus. Unable to swallow liquids; actively spitting out pooling saliva. Reports a chronic history of intermittent solid food dysphagia, describing a sensation of food slowly traversing the oesophagus over the past year. Notes upper abdominal pain and heartburn completely refractory to high-dose proton pump inhibitor therapy. Personal history of severe allergic rhinitis and childhood eczema.
Functional Status: Pacing anxiously, holding a cup to catch drooled saliva.
Social/Safety Concerns: High anxiety; risk of aspiration pneumonia if impaction is not cleared.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 145/90 (Pain/Anxiety) | HR: 102 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Patient is in acute distress, expectorating clear frothy saliva frequently. No stridor, wheeze, or voice change.
Physical Exam: Oropharynx is clear without visible foreign body, erythema, or swelling; macroscopic appearance is entirely normal. Neck is supple without crepitus or masses. Lungs are clear to auscultation bilaterally, ruling out gross aspiration. Abdomen is soft and non-tender. The clinical presentation is a textbook demonstration of solid food dysphagia/impaction in a patient with a strong atopic history; characteristic stacked circular rings and eosinophilic infiltration on biopsy are strongly suspected pending endoscopy.
Environmental Observations: A partially eaten meal remains on the dining table.
Medication Reconciliation: Taking daily oral antihistamines and Esomeprazole 40mg with no improvement in underlying dysphagia symptoms.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Eosinophilic Oesophagitis - Status: Exacerbated (Acute oesophageal food bolus obstruction)
Differentials:

Peptic Stricture (Barrett's) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The patient's prominent atopic history and the fact that symptoms are completely refractory to high-dose PPI therapy, which heavily favors an allergic/eosinophilic aetiology over a purely acid-driven stricture.

Achalasia - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The dysphagia being primarily localized to solid foods rather than equal difficulty with both liquids and solids, and the absence of a dilated oesophagus producing massive regurgitation of undigested, hours-old food.

Oesophageal Malignancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The younger age, strong atopic history, intermittent nature of symptoms prior to this acute total blockage, and lack of unintentional profound weight loss.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with an acute, complete oesophageal obstruction secondary to a food bolus impaction, occurring against a background of chronic intermittent dysphagia and atopy. The inability to manage oral secretions (spitting saliva) indicates a total mechanical blockade. Urgent out-of-hours evaluation was required to confirm airway safety, ensure no signs of oesophageal perforation (crepitus, shock), and administer an initial trial of pharmacological relaxants (e.g., effervescent agents/glucagon). As the bolus failed to pass, immediate referral for emergency endoscopy was mandated to physically extract the food before ischaemic necrosis of the oesophageal wall occurred, a risk that precluded waiting for normal business hours.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered oral effervescent granules (citric acid/simethicone) in a small water sip to attempt mechanical dislodgement, which was unsuccessful. Facilitated immediate transfer to the Emergency Department for urgent therapeutic endoscopy.
Positive Behavioral Change: When eating any future solid meals, explicitly put your fork down between every single bite and chew the food until it is entirely a liquid paste before swallowing. This mechanical breakdown drastically reduces the size of the bolus, physically preventing it from wedging into the narrowed, inflamed rings of your oesophagus.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: GP review 3 days post-endoscopy to review biopsy results and initiate topical corticosteroid therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to ED is required. If the patient suddenly develops a harsh, barking cough, difficulty breathing, or severe, tearing pain radiating straight through to the back during transport, the partner must call 000 immediately as this suggests aspiration or perforation.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The underlying cause of the narrowing must be confirmed by taking tiny tissue samples during the camera test.
Clinical Course: The blockage must be removed mechanically tonight. Long-term treatment will require swallowed asthma inhalers to coat the food pipe and reduce the allergic inflammation.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Do not attempt to 'wash down' the blockage by chugging large amounts of water. The tube is completely blocked, and the fluid will simply back up into your throat and spill into your lungs, causing severe pneumonia.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Your body is having an allergic reaction inside your feeding tube, causing it to become stiff and form tight, fibrous rings. The piece of meat has become physically wedged in one of these rings. The hospital will pass a small camera down your throat while you are asleep to physically grab the food and pull it out safely.
Actionable Steps: Continue spitting your saliva into the cup. Swallowing your saliva will only increase the pressure column above the blockage, worsening the chest pain and increasing the risk of fluid overflowing into your windpipe.

